{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROOT CAUSE PROVEN: THE LOCK BUMP IS IMPOSSIBLE. THE CAP COMES FROM inspect-ai, NOT FROM US.\n\nThis settles which remediation option is available, so it should not be re-litigated. Established by\nDIRECT EXPERIMENT, not inference: I temporarily added 'constraint-dependencies = [\"click>=8.3.3\"]'\nunder [tool.uv] and ran 'uv lock'. It is UNSATISFIABLE, and uv named the culprit:\n\n    No solution found when resolving dependencies for split\n    (python_full_version >= '3.14' and sys_platform == 'win32'):\n    Because inspect-ai>=0.3.221 depends on one of:\n        click>=8.1.3,<8.2.0\n        click>8.2.0,<8.2.2\n    and click>=8.3.3, we can conclude that inspect-ai>=0.3.221 cannot be used.\n\nSo inspect-ai CAPS click below 8.2.2 across the available versions (it enumerated up to 0.3.242).\ninspect-ai arrives via the [eval] extra, and uv's UNIVERSAL resolution locks every extra jointly —\nthe mechanism pyproject.toml:250-256 already documents for the boto3 diamond. The working tree was\nrestored immediately afterwards; 'git status --porcelain' is clean and neither pyproject.toml nor\nuv.lock is modified.\n\nWHY THE EARLIER ATTEMPTS LOOKED INERT. 'uv lock --upgrade-package click' (and with\n--refresh-package) reported success and left 8.2.1, changing only an unrelated zipfile-zstd marker.\nThat is NOT a stale cache and NOT the uv-version mismatch I had hypothesised: --upgrade-package only\nPERMITS a move within the existing constraint set, and 8.2.1 remains the newest click that satisfies\ninspect-ai's cap. So the no-op was the resolver being correct, and my resolver-version hypothesis is\nRETRACTED — recording that rather than leaving a wrong lead in the ticket.\n\nTHEREFORE OPTION 1 (minimal lock bump to >= 8.3.3) IS RULED OUT. It cannot be done without\nremoving or downgrading inspect-ai, i.e. dropping the [eval] extra's framework, which is far more\ninvasive than the advisory warrants and is not this ticket's business.\n\nOPTION 2 IS THE PATH, AND IT IS NOW GENUINELY JUSTIFIED RATHER THAN A SHRUG. The justification has\nthree legs, each verifiable:\n 1. click is a TRANSITIVE dependency — 'git grep \"import click\"' over src returns NOTHING; rebar\n    never imports it.\n 2. PYSEC-2026-2132 is a command-injection in click.edit() specifically, and 'click.edit' appears\n    NOWHERE in src, tests or scripts. The vulnerable code path is unreachable in this project.\n 3. The cap is UPSTREAM and outside our control: inspect-ai pins click < 8.2.2. We cannot fix this by\n    choosing better versions.\nSo the ignore is not 'we would rather not deal with it' — it is 'the advisory does not apply to this\nproject, and the version that fixes it is unreachable while inspect-ai is a dependency'.\n\nREQUIRED SHAPE OF THE IGNORE, so it cannot rot into a blanket suppression:\n * scope it to the ONE advisory id (--ignore-vuln PYSEC-2026-2132), never a broad skip, and keep the\n   pip-audit job GATING;\n * an inline comment recording all three legs above AND a REMOVE-WHEN condition: remove this ignore\n   as soon as inspect-ai relaxes its click cap enough for the lock to carry click >= 8.3.3\n   (re-test with the constraint experiment above — it is cheap and decisive);\n * greppable, so the next person auditing suppressions finds it with the advisory id.\n\nWORTH FILING SEPARATELY (not here, and not blocking): nothing currently tells us WHEN inspect-ai\nrelaxes that cap, so the ignore could outlive its justification silently. A periodic re-test — the\nsame one-line constraint experiment — would catch it. That is a hygiene follow-up, not part of\nunblocking."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785712644415668001,"uuid":"df84efa1-2b04-4460-b579-585237f9345a"}